course: everyday
lesson: 440
title: April Fools' Day
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: On the first of April, Dad in Dublin told the kids school was cancelled because of snow. They cheered — then looked outside: sunshine. «April Fools, ye eejits!» («Ye» is Irish for «you lot», and an eejit is a fool.) April Fools' Day, 1 April, is a day in many countries when people play harmless tricks and tell silly made-up news, then shout «April Fools!». In Britain and Ireland an old rule says the tricks stop at midday: when Liam's friend tried one in the afternoon, Liam said «Too late — after noon, you're the fool!»
explain: Even grown-ups get fooled. In 1957 a BBC television programme showed Swiss farmers picking spaghetti from trees, and many viewers believed it; people rang to ask how to grow their own spaghetti tree. It is still called one of the best April Fools ever. Companies join in too: one announced a «pizza-flavoured toothpaste», lots of people shared it, and some weren't sure it was a joke — which got the company plenty of attention. Some April jokes are adverts in disguise.
explain: Good April jokes are silly and quick to undo. Kai's teacher in Sydney announced «a surprise test worth half your grade», then said «April Fools!» and handed out lollies (sweets). Jade in Wales put a fake «wet paint» sign on her brother's chair, and he stood all through dinner while Mum laughed into her cawl, a Welsh stew; next day he put the sign on Jade's chair. Ella's grandpa in Texas swears every year he's seen a jackalope — a made-up rabbit with antlers — and shows a stuffed toy as «proof». Now Ella helps fool the little ones.
explain: Bad April jokes are scary or cost people something. Mia in Toronto told her mum she'd broken her arm; Mum panicked and grabbed the car keys, and when Mia shouted «April Fools!», Mum didn't laugh: «Don't joke about getting hurt. It makes my heart stop.» Sam told his class that Mr Hughes was leaving the school forever, and a girl started crying. «Next time, maybe something without tears.» Arjun's friend messaged «Free iPhones at the mall, first 100 people!»; some parents drove kids there for nothing and were annoyed. A joke that spreads like that acts just like a scam.
fact: April Fools' Day is 1 April, when people in many countries play harmless tricks and tell made-up news.
fact: In Britain and Ireland tradition says April Fools' tricks should stop at midday, and someone who plays one after noon is the fool.
fact: In 1957 the BBC programme Panorama showed a spoof film of spaghetti growing on trees, and many viewers believed it.
fact: Companies often use 1 April for joke products or news, which also gets them attention.
fact: A good April joke is silly, not scary, and is revealed quickly.
fact: Never fake an injury or emergency as a joke; it frightens the people who love you.
fact: Before a joke, ask yourself: will anyone be sad or hurt? If yes, pick another.
fact: Joke messages about free prizes can make people travel or spend for nothing, just like a scam.
fact: On 1 April, double-check surprising news before believing or sharing it.
fact: The jackalope is a made-up animal, a rabbit with antlers, from American folk tales.
fact: Being fooled is fine; laughing and fooling them back is part of the fun.
fact: «Ye» (Ireland) means you, often more than one; «lollies» (Australia) are sweets; «cawl» is a Welsh stew.
exercise: What date is April Fools' Day? | 1 April | 1 May | 31 March | 4 July
exercise: Why didn't Dad's snow story in Dublin fool the kids for long? | It was sunny outside | Snow is very common in Dublin in April | School really was closed that day | Their teacher rang to say it was a joke
exercise: What did the famous BBC April Fools' film show? | Spaghetti growing on trees | Penguins flying south over the sea | Pizza growing in fields | Cows that could talk
exercise: What did many viewers do after the spaghetti film? | Rang to ask how to grow spaghetti trees | Complained that spaghetti is fake | Planted dry spaghetti in their gardens at once | Ignored it completely
exercise: Why did Mia's mum not laugh at the broken-arm joke? | Faking an injury frightened her badly | She didn't hear the «April Fools» bit at the end | She was busy cooking | She thought it was a bad pun
exercise: What did Kai's teacher give the class after her fake test? | Lollies | Extra homework | A real test | A day off
exercise: What did one company announce on April Fools' Day? | Pizza-flavoured toothpaste | A free car for every family | A new planet | A toothbrush that can talk
exercise: Why do companies make April Fools' jokes? | They get attention, like an advert | They have to, because the law says so | To trick people into paying for nothing | Because the shops are shut that day
exercise: Why was Sam's joke about Mr Hughes a bad one? | It made a girl cry | Mr Hughes really had left the school | It wasn't funny enough | It was told after midday
exercise: What is a jackalope? | A made-up rabbit with antlers | A real deer that lives in Texas | A type of desert cactus | A kind of cowboy hat
exercise: What does Ella do with the jackalope joke now? | Helps fool the little ones | Tells everyone straight away it's fake | Hunts for a real one in the desert | Hides the stuffed toy in the attic
exercise: What did Liam say when his friend pranked him after noon? | «Too late, after noon you're the fool!» | «Good one, you really got me there!» | «April Fools is cancelled» | «That's not fair»
exercise: What happened after the «free iPhones» message? | Some parents drove kids there for nothing | Everyone who went got a free iPhone and a phone case | The mall gave out sweets | Nobody went at all
exercise: Why is the «free iPhones» joke like a scam? | It makes people travel and waste time for nothing | It asks for your passwords, your address and bank details | It comes from a company | It's sent late at night
exercise: What did Jade's brother do after the «wet paint» joke? | Put the sign on Jade's chair next day | Painted Jade's chair bright green for real | Told Mum to punish her | Never sat down again
exercise: What should you do with surprising news on 1 April? | Double-check it before believing or sharing | Share it as fast as you can with everyone you know, just in case | Believe it because it's news | Ignore all news that day
test: Dad: «April Fools, ye eejits!» Is he angry? | No, he's teasing them for being fooled | Yes, he's furious and shouting at them | He's calling them clever | He's asking them to leave
test: Your mate pranks you at 3 pm on 1 April. What does the British and Irish tradition say? | After noon, the prankster is the fool | Pranks can go on all night until midnight | Pranks only count after noon | The day after is April Fools
test: Kid: «gonna text mum I fell off my bike, then April Fools lol». Good idea? | No — never fake an injury, it terrifies people | Yes, it's the funniest joke there is | Yes, as long as you say «April Fools» fast | Only if Dad's home too
test: Teacher in Sydney: «Surprise test, half your grade!» then «April Fools!» and lollies. What are lollies? | Sweets | Lollipop ladies | Pencils | Stickers
test: Grandpa shows a stuffed jackalope. Which is true? | It's a made-up animal and a family joke | Jackalopes really live in Texas deserts | It's a real rare rabbit | Jackalopes have no ears
test: A viral post on 1 April: «Pizza toothpaste out now!!». What's the smart move? | Check the date and whether it makes sense before sharing | Share it with everyone fast | Buy some at once | Believe it because it's online
test: What would have happened if Sam had joked «Mr Hughes is wearing odd socks» instead? | People could laugh with nobody sad | A girl would still have cried | Mr Hughes would leave the school for good, just like the joke said | The class would be punished
test: Jade's brother stood through dinner because of the «wet paint» sign. What simple check would have saved him? | Touching or looking at the chair — no smell, no wet | Asking Jade if it's true | Calling a painter to come and check it for him | Reading the sign twice, slowly
test: «FREE iPhones at the mall, first 100!!» Friend says it's a joke, but people drove there. What's the lesson? | Even jokes can act like scams — don't share what you're not sure of | Malls always give free iPhones on 1 April to the first hundred people | Only people who drove are silly | Jokes never hurt anyone
test: Your gran falls for your harmless April joke and laughs. Is it okay to be fooled? | Yes, being fooled is part of the fun | No, being fooled is always shameful, even for grans | Only if you're a child | Only after noon
test: In a cartoon a kid fakes an emergency on April Fools and everyone laughs at the end. What is truer in real life? | Faking emergencies frightens people and isn't funny | Everyone always laughs in the end | Emergencies make the best jokes | It's fine on 1 April only, never on other days
exam: Identify the date upon which April Fools' Day is customarily observed. | The first day of April | The last day of March | The first day of May | The fourteenth day of April
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the BBC broadcast of 1957 depicting the cultivation of spaghetti upon trees. | It was a spoof which numerous viewers believed | It was a genuine agricultural report from Switzerland | It was disregarded by all viewers | It was broadcast in error
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the feigning of an injury by way of an April Fools' jest? | It is unsuitable, occasioning fear in those who care for the child | It is the most suitable form of jest | It is permissible prior to midday, in accordance with tradition | It is harmless if subsequently disclosed
exam: It is given that a jest announcing free telephones caused families to travel to a shopping centre to no purpose. Determine the statement most consistent with the established facts. | The jest operated in the manner of a scam, occasioning loss of time and expense | The jest was entirely harmless | The families were obliged to share the jest | The shopping centre was liable to supply the telephones as announced
